{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-07_16-54-34-swiftpaxos_epaxos-real-run/draft && /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nsub={\\\"action\\\":\\\"review\\\",\n \\\"rationale\\\":\\\"Two items in one review product: the correspondence of the new artifact for claim C3-applied-effect-follows-committed-vector (52a176deb66c4588a59d71f8afeb13ab, executed as ef136d1e25924ce59ccedc4fe9e2af2e), and the resolution of the open issue ef6b54abce294555b31a3c9a6ca606ac that my earlier scope challenge raised against the C1 artifact which asserted the same applied-state property.\\\",\n \\\"artifact_id\\\":\\\"52a176deb66c4588a59d71f8afeb13ab\\\",\n \\\"review_items\\\":[{\n  \\\"aspect\\\":\\\"checker_correspondence\\\",\n  \\\"status\\\":\\\"no_issue_found\\\",\n  \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"S20-exec\\\",\\\"S23-state-conflict-apply\\\",\\\"S13-handleCommit\\\",\\\"S15-handlePrepareReply\\\"],\n  \\\"report_title\\\":\\\"同一实例在不同副本产生不同的已应用效果\\\",\n  \\\"report_answer\\\":\\\"每个副本用自己的已提交记录执行后，对端状态机读到 04，而恢复者与第三副本读到 (void)，applied_match=false：同一实例在不同副本的状态机效果不同，说明记录层的不一致确实成为可观察的应用层分叉。该结论属于新义务 C3（其范围包含执行该实例），用于确认 C3；它不用于确认 C1。\\\",\n  \\\"rationale\\\":\\\"Requirement versus predicate: claim C3 requires that replicas holding the same instance committed apply the same effect for the instance's key, and the property asserts applied_match on the applied_state_divergence event; the retained run shows peer_value 04 against recoverer_value and third_value (void) with applied_match false, and the same event prints the command each replica held, so the applied difference is tied to the different committed vectors rather than to an unstated assumption. Entry and caller duties: the harness drives the recovery, the accept round and the Commit broadcast as before, then applies each record with Exec.executeCommand and probes the store with a GET command; the claim's scope now includes applying the instance and excludes the production loop, watermarks, timing and client-visible replies, and the harness substitutions are declared in the plan's semantic changes and uncertainties. Oracle and identity: the three printed values are read from each replica's replicated store for one key, the claim fixes the key as the one the committed commands touch, and the instance identity is shared with the prerequisite reply, so correlation is monotonic and the comparison is not derived from a context expected to change. Legal history: the committed records come from the same driven path as the confirmed C1 checks (recoverer and third replica NOOP, peer PUT), so the input is the observed consequence of the confirmed defect rather than a fabricated state. Contrary evidence and alternatives: the alternative reading that execution lies outside any obligation about adopting a value is exactly why this proposition is its own claim; the reservations that remain are recorded as limitations, namely direct execution instead of the production loop, one instance and one key, and the prepared committed record. The item therefore clears correspondence for C3 rather than extending C1.\\\",\n  \\\"limitations\\\":[\\\"the harness applies records directly with Exec.executeCommand, so scheduling, watermark advancement and the recovery timeout are outside this check\\\",\n   \\\"one instance and one key are compared; other keys and repeated instances are not\\\",\n   \\\"the committed records are prepared/driven as in the C1 checks, so the history that produced them is not constructed\\\"]}],\n \\\"resolutions\\\":[{\n  \\\"issue_id\\\":\\\"ef6b54abce294555b31a3c9a6ca606ac\\\",\n  \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"S20-exec\\\",\\\"S23-state-conflict-apply\\\",\\\"S15-handlePrepareReply\\\"],\n  \\\"evidence_ids\\\":[\\\"52a176deb66c4588a59d71f8afeb13ab\\\",\\\"ef136d1e25924ce59ccedc4fe9e2af2e\\\"],\n  \\\"rationale\\\":\\\"The issue was that the C1 artifact asserted an applied-state property while the accepted C1 scope excludes execution. That proposition is now fixed as its own accepted obligation, C3-applied-effect-follows-committed-vector, whose scope includes applying the instance and whose first check (artifact 52a176deb66c4588a59d71f8afeb13ab, execution ef136d1e25924ce59ccedc4fe9e2af2e) asserts the same predicate on the same observation. The resolution is therefore that the applied-state finding belongs to C3 and must not be used to confirm C1; the disputed C1 artifact's other properties were already reviewed on their own terms, and no C1 result depends on the applied-state property.\\\",\n  \\\"residual_issue_ids\\\":[],\n  \\\"scope_limitations\\\":[\\\"the C1 artifact that asserted the applied-state property keeps it in its fixed bytes; this resolution changes attribution, not that artifact\\\",\n    \\\"C3's own check carries the limitations recorded in the review item above (direct execution, one instance and key, prepared records)\\\"]}]}\njson.dump(sub,open('submission-review-c3.json','w'),indent=2,ensure_ascii=False)\nprint('ok')\nPY\ncd .. && /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -I -B -c \\\"import sys; sys.path.insert(0, '/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src'); from consensus_assurance.cli import main; raise SystemExit(main())\\\" validate --run /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-07_16-54-34-swiftpaxos_epaxos-real-run --submission submission-review-c3.json 2>&1 | head -14\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
